# Build context for the single-container image (the root `Dockerfile`).
#
# The root Dockerfile builds the web app in a `node:22-alpine` stage, runs
# `npm ci`, and then does `COPY frontend/ ./`. Without this file that `COPY`
# carries the developer's `node_modules` (713 MB) and `.next` (345 MB) into
# the stage and lays them over the install the container just made — so the
# image ships the host's dependency tree, built for the host's libc, instead
# of the lockfile's for Alpine's musl. See `frontend/.dockerignore` for the
# same problem in the other direction.
#
# It also keeps the context from carrying the whole working tree.
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Nothing in the image is built from these.
docs
android
api
assets
scripts
uploads

# Host-installed dependencies and build output, at any depth.
**/node_modules
**/.next
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/coverage

# Local state: the SQLite database, the uploads directory, virtualenvs.
backend/data
backend/.venv
backend/uploads
worker/.venv
# Local environment files, at any depth. These patterns have to be anchored
# with `**/`: a bare `.env` matches only the root of the build context, and
# this Dockerfile copies `backend/` and `frontend/` wholesale — so a
# developer's `backend/.env` (which `Settings` loads from the working
# directory) or `frontend/.env.local` would otherwise be baked into the
# published image, with whatever secrets they hold.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Private keys and certificates. The session signing key is written under
# `backend/data/` at runtime, but a stray `.pem` in the working tree must not
# be able to reach an image.
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
